<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acts of Union 1707</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Acts of Union</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Acts of Union 1707 were the parliamentary acts that united the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> in 1707. They created a single parliament and a common market, opening English colonial trade to Scots. The union is treated as a foundational step for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707.jpg" alt="Acts of Union 1707" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_England_(1702%E2%80%931707).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> shared a monarch from 1603, when James VI of Scotland inherited the English throne from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> and became James VI and I. He announced an intention to unite the two realms, but the English Parliament feared an absolutist structure like Scotland's, and he withdrew his proposals while taking the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. Union was tried again in 1606, 1667 and 1689. Religion kept the churches apart: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> was Calvinist, and attempts by James and Charles I to impose policy led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a> from 1639. Some Presbyterian leaders later saw union as a way to secure free trade and a Presbyterian kirk.</p><p>By the early eighteenth century the Scottish economy was weak: largely agrarian, with chronic deflation and tariff wars over linen. Colonial trade led to the failed Darien scheme. Queen Anne had favoured deeper integration since she came to the throne in 1702, and in 1705 both parliaments agreed to new negotiations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Each country named 31 commissioners, led on the Scottish side by the Duke of Queensberry and the Earl of Seafield. Most Scots favoured union, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s included many Whigs and only one Tory. They met at the Cockpit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 16 April to 22 July 1706. Within days England had a guarantee that the Hanoverian dynasty would succeed Anne to the Scottish crown, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> had a guarantee of access to colonial markets.</p><p>In the Scottish Parliament the debate began on 3 October 1706. About 100 of its 227 members supported the Court Party, aided by some 25 of the Squadrone Volante. The opposing Country party included the Duke of Hamilton, Lord Belhaven and Andrew Fletcher of Saltoun. The Court party had significant funding from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and included many in debt after the Darien Disaster. Article 1 passed by 116 votes to 83 on 4 November 1706, and the treaty as a whole was ratified on 16 January 1707 by 110 votes to 69. The Parliament of England then passed an equivalent act, and both took effect on 1 May 1707.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Treaty of Union had 25 articles, 15 of them economic. It joined the two kingdoms in an incorporating union as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, with Queen Anne as sovereign and one parliament at Westminster, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> sent representative peers to the House of Lords. It created a customs and monetary union and restated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a>, barring Roman Catholics from the throne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> remained the established church, the Court of Session stayed within Scotland and Scots law remained in force. A separate act secured the Presbyterian church, after which the church ended its open opposition. In 1708 English treason law was extended across Great Britain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acts of Union 1707” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Acts of Union</b> of 1707, which united England and Scotland into the Kingdom of Great Britain under a single Parliament, transformed the constitution: Scottish commercial energy and manpower combined with English colonial access to create a more powerful imperial engine than either kingdom had possessed alone.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Acts of Union</b>, ratified by the Scottish Parliament in January 1707 and by the English Parliament in March 1707, created the Kingdom of Great Britain on 1 May 1707, ending England’s existence as a separate state, although its law, church and identity survived within the new kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Acts of Union</b> took effect on 1 May 1707, when Queen Anne became the first monarch of Great Britain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After negotiations in 1706, the Parliament of Scotland ratified the <b>Treaty of Union</b>, and on 1 May 1707 Scotland and England were united as the Kingdom of Great Britain, with one parliament at Westminster.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The loss of capital weakened Scottish resistance to union, and the 1707 <b>Act of Union</b> included compensation for the company's investors.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-caledonia-darien">New Caledonia (Darien)</a></li></ul></section>
